WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.572
The CrestApps Commerce modules let an Orchard Core site take payments.

00:00:05.572 --> 00:00:11.045
Sell subscriptions online, set up payment plans for your customers, send invoices and

00:00:11.045 --> 00:00:13.933
receipts, and keep track of every payment.

00:00:13.933 --> 00:00:19.286
This video walks through each part, from connecting a payment provider to extending

00:00:19.286 --> 00:00:20.850
the modules in code.

00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:29.859
Selling online and selling over the phone often end up in different places:

00:00:29.859 --> 00:00:34.160
a payment page here, and a spreadsheet of who still owes what over there.

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:40.584
The Commerce modules send every payment through one checkout, whether a visitor buys

00:00:40.584 --> 00:00:45.690
a plan, a customer pays an invoice, or a saved card is charged on schedule.

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:50.592
Every amount owed is a transaction, so there is one list of what

00:00:50.592 --> 00:00:55.100
each customer owes and has paid, with reminders and receipts built in.

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:01.342
The checkout takes the money.

00:01:01.342 --> 00:01:03.500
Payment providers plug into it:

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:08.983
Stripe for cards, Apple Pay and Google Pay, and Pay Later for customers who pay offline.

00:01:08.983 --> 00:01:11.900
Coupons and taxes are applied along the way.

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:16.132
Transactions record what customers owe and pay.

00:01:16.132 --> 00:01:21.716
Reminders, numbered invoices with a link that pays them, and receipts are built

00:01:21.716 --> 00:01:26.380
on them, and the Payments and Refunds screens show every attempt.

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:29.391
On top, you choose what to sell:

00:01:29.391 --> 00:01:35.817
Subscriptions for plans that renew, Subscriptions Sites for ready-made websites, and Installment Plans

00:01:35.817 --> 00:01:39.030
for payment plans that an administrator sets up.

00:01:43.207 --> 00:01:47.848
Under Settings, Payments, Stripe, paste your Stripe keys and save them.

00:01:47.848 --> 00:01:52.067
Then click Connect, which links your Stripe account to the site.

00:01:52.519 --> 00:01:56.735
While Enable Production is cleared, Stripe runs in test mode:

00:01:56.735 --> 00:02:01.139
you can try every flow with test cards, and no real money moves.

00:02:01.593 --> 00:02:05.774
Under Commerce, Currencies, choose the currencies you sell in.

00:02:05.774 --> 00:02:09.463
Plans and products pick their currency from this list.

00:02:13.657 --> 00:02:16.749
Under Commerce, Coupons, click Add coupon.

00:02:16.749 --> 00:02:21.647
Give it a code, a description, and a percentage or a fixed amount off.

00:02:22.306 --> 00:02:26.466
Choose what it applies to, such as the first billing cycle, how many

00:02:26.466 --> 00:02:28.997
times it can be used, and when it ends.

00:02:28.997 --> 00:02:30.346
Then click Create.

00:02:30.824 --> 00:02:35.352
The list shows each coupon with its discount, how often it was used,

00:02:35.352 --> 00:02:36.760
and its end date.

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:39.514
Filter it by status in the list header.

00:02:43.716 --> 00:02:47.281
Visitors find your plans on the Service Plans page.

00:02:47.281 --> 00:02:51.845
Each plan shows its price, its billing period, and any setup fee or

00:02:51.845 --> 00:02:54.346
free trial, with a Sign up button.

00:02:54.797 --> 00:02:57.824
The checkout walks the buyer through its steps.

00:02:57.824 --> 00:03:03.077
Here they create an account, while the order summary shows the plan and its setup fee.

00:03:13.082 --> 00:03:17.812
On the payment step, they enter their card, and can apply a promotion code.

00:03:21.344 --> 00:03:25.401
Stripe confirms the payment, and the purchase is complete.

00:03:25.401 --> 00:03:27.684
The subscriber gets a receipt.

00:03:29.268 --> 00:03:34.894
Administrators find every subscriber under Subscriptions, Agreements, with its status.

00:03:34.894 --> 00:03:39.418
Each agreement shows the plan, its billing, and what happened to it.

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:45.085
Under Actions, Cancel subscription stops the billing, right away or when the paid

00:03:45.085 --> 00:03:48.442
period ends, and Pause billing holds the payments.

00:03:48.442 --> 00:03:50.720
Both ask for confirmation first.

00:03:54.909 --> 00:03:59.289
With Subscriptions Sites, a plan can sell a whole Orchard Core site.

00:03:59.289 --> 00:04:03.668
Its checkout adds a step where the buyer names the new site, chooses

00:04:03.668 --> 00:04:06.569
its address, and creates its administrator.

00:04:08.064 --> 00:04:12.113
Everything they can still fix is checked here, before they pay:

00:04:12.113 --> 00:04:16.514
a site name, an address or a domain that is already taken is refused.

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:26.750
They pay by card, and the purchase is complete.

00:04:31.917 --> 00:04:36.617
The site is created right after, in the background, so a slow setup

00:04:36.617 --> 00:04:38.227
never loses a sale.

00:04:39.813 --> 00:04:43.752
Under My Sites, the customer sees their site being created.

00:04:43.752 --> 00:04:47.995
Once it is ready, Open takes them straight to it, already set up

00:04:47.995 --> 00:04:49.753
from the plan's recipe.

00:04:51.337 --> 00:04:57.422
Administrators follow every site under Subscriptions, Site provisioning, and can retry one that

00:04:57.422 --> 00:04:59.711
failed, once the cause is fixed.

00:04:59.711 --> 00:05:03.507
The site runs for as long as the subscription is current.

00:05:07.689 --> 00:05:12.605
Installment plans sell something on a payment plan, for example over the phone.

00:05:12.605 --> 00:05:17.925
Under Subscriptions, Installment Plans, click New plan, and pick a customer, or create

00:05:17.925 --> 00:05:19.949
one from a name and an email.

00:05:20.401 --> 00:05:24.354
Enter what the plan is for, the total, the down payment, the number

00:05:24.354 --> 00:05:28.469
of payments after it, how often they fall due, and the first due date.

00:05:28.469 --> 00:05:30.581
The schedule updates as you type.

00:05:31.031 --> 00:05:33.604
Choose how the payments are collected:

00:05:33.604 --> 00:05:38.342
charge the card on each due date, or invoice the customer for each payment.

00:05:38.342 --> 00:05:40.681
Then continue to the down payment.

00:05:41.284 --> 00:05:43.331
Enter the customer's card.

00:05:43.331 --> 00:05:48.258
The down payment is charged now, and the card is kept for the schedule.

00:05:48.258 --> 00:05:53.184
On devices that support them, Apple Pay and Google Pay appear here too.

00:05:53.634 --> 00:05:55.146
The plan is active.

00:05:55.146 --> 00:05:59.561
Its page shows every payment and where it stands, the card on file,

00:05:59.561 --> 00:06:01.314
and the plan's history.

00:06:05.497 --> 00:06:11.364
The list shows every plan with its customer, what was received, and the next payment.

00:06:11.364 --> 00:06:15.747
Search it, or filter it by status, such as Active or Past due.

00:06:16.199 --> 00:06:18.987
Each payment is charged on its due date.

00:06:18.987 --> 00:06:23.291
A declined card is tried again after a few days, and the customer

00:06:23.291 --> 00:06:24.745
is told each time.

00:06:24.745 --> 00:06:28.989
After the last try, the payment is left for the customer to pay.

00:06:29.443 --> 00:06:34.942
Charge now collects a payment early, or retries a declined card straight away.

00:06:34.942 --> 00:06:37.823
It always asks for confirmation first.

00:06:38.273 --> 00:06:43.331
Cancel the plan stops the payments not yet received, and tells the customer.

00:06:43.331 --> 00:06:47.463
Payments already received stay received, and can be refunded.

00:06:51.642 --> 00:06:58.072
Every payment of a plan, every Pay Later order, and any other balance is a transaction.

00:06:58.072 --> 00:07:04.432
Commerce, Transactions lists them, and the filters narrow them by status or by source.

00:07:04.884 --> 00:07:09.449
A transaction shows its total, what was paid, and what is outstanding, with

00:07:09.449 --> 00:07:12.324
a timeline of everything that happened to it.

00:07:12.783 --> 00:07:18.452
Record a payment taken by cash or bank transfer, mark it as paid, or cancel it.

00:07:18.452 --> 00:07:21.253
Each of these asks for confirmation.

00:07:21.704 --> 00:07:27.381
Reminders tell customers before a payment falls due, and again while it is overdue.

00:07:27.381 --> 00:07:31.974
Under Settings, Commerce, Transactions, choose when they are sent.

00:07:36.175 --> 00:07:39.450
A payment the customer pays themselves is invoiced.

00:07:39.450 --> 00:07:43.815
The first time they are told about it, it gets a short invoice number.

00:07:43.815 --> 00:07:45.825
Send reminder tells them now.

00:07:46.279 --> 00:07:48.711
The invoice is ready to print.

00:07:48.711 --> 00:07:54.115
Its payment link pays it without signing in, and expires after sixty days.

00:07:54.115 --> 00:07:56.479
Copy it, to send it yourself.

00:07:58.069 --> 00:08:01.931
The customer opens the link, sees the invoice, and clicks Pay.

00:08:01.931 --> 00:08:06.929
The checkout opens straight on the payment, with the balance in the order summary.

00:08:07.379 --> 00:08:11.049
They enter their card, just like in any other checkout.

00:08:13.995 --> 00:08:19.295
The transaction is settled, and a receipt with its own short number is sent to them.

00:08:23.468 --> 00:08:28.769
Commerce, Payments lists every attempt to take money, successful or not, and why

00:08:28.769 --> 00:08:30.618
a failed payment failed.

00:08:33.013 --> 00:08:37.885
Refund sends money back to the card it came from, in full or in part.

00:08:37.885 --> 00:08:43.213
It asks for confirmation, and the customer is told when the refund succeeds.

00:08:43.663 --> 00:08:46.777
Commerce, Refunds lists every refund.

00:08:46.777 --> 00:08:52.643
A refund that the gateway can't finish on its own is settled by hand, here.

00:08:56.843 --> 00:08:59.174
The plan defaults are site settings:

00:08:59.174 --> 00:09:03.058
how a new plan collects its payments, and the days after which a

00:09:03.058 --> 00:09:05.000
declined card is tried again.

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:07.663
A recipe sets them with the Settings step.

00:09:08.413 --> 00:09:13.773
Set the site's Base URL, so the links in reminders point at your public address.

00:09:13.773 --> 00:09:19.569
A background task collects the scheduled payments every fifteen minutes, and on several

00:09:19.569 --> 00:09:23.993
servers, a distributed lock, such as Redis, keeps each plan safe.

00:09:28.193 --> 00:09:32.940
Developers create and manage plans with IInstallmentPlanService:

00:09:32.940 --> 00:09:35.720
create, charge, process and cancel.

00:09:35.720 --> 00:09:39.043
Money only ever moves through the checkout.

00:09:39.793 --> 00:09:44.163
To react to a payment, implement ITransactionPaymentHandler.

00:09:44.163 --> 00:09:49.659
It runs once for every payment applied to a transaction, however it was paid.

00:09:49.659 --> 00:09:52.373
Refunds have IPaymentRefundHandler.

00:09:53.123 --> 00:09:56.340
Any payment provider can take installment plans.

00:09:56.340 --> 00:10:00.688
It keeps a card when asked, charges it without the payer, and reads

00:10:00.688 --> 00:10:04.143
it back through ICheckoutSavedPaymentMethodProvider.

00:10:04.893 --> 00:10:07.436
The checkout isn't only for these modules.

00:10:07.436 --> 00:10:09.342
Any module can take a payment:

00:10:09.342 --> 00:10:13.792
start a checkout with ICheckoutEngine for what is being bought, and finish the

00:10:13.792 --> 00:10:17.393
purchase in an ICheckoutHandler once the payment is confirmed.

00:10:19.393 --> 00:10:24.510
The CrestApps Commerce modules take every payment through one checkout, and keep one

00:10:24.510 --> 00:10:27.295
record of what each customer owes and paid.

00:10:27.295 --> 00:10:28.773
Thanks for watching.

